[The Start of Assistant 1’s Answer]
No, medieval cartographers did not regularly write "here be dragons" on their maps. This phrase can only be found on the Hunt-Lenox Globe and the Ostrich Egg Globe, both next to a coast in Southeast Asia. Instead, maps from this era were more likely to have "here are lions" inscribed on them. Maps in this period did occasionally feature illustrations of mythical beasts like dragons and sea serpents, as well as exotic animals like elephants, which may have contributed to the misconception.
[The End of Assistant 1’s Answer]
[The Start of Assistant 2’s Answer]
The phrase "here be dragons" is often associated with medieval maps, but it is unclear whether cartographers actually used it regularly. While some maps do contain this phrase or similar warnings about unknown territories, there is no definitive evidence that it was a standard practice among medieval mapmakers.

The origin of the phrase "here be dragons" is uncertain, but one popular theory suggests that it comes from the Latin "hic sunt dracones," which means "here are dragons." This phrase was possibly added to maps by early cartographers to warn sailors and explorers of the dangers lurking in uncharted waters or unexplored lands.

However, it is important to note that many modern historians and scholars argue that the phrase "here be dragons" was more likely a later addition to maps, added by map collectors and enthusiasts rather than the original cartographers. They point out that few genuine medieval maps contain this phrase, and those that do often have questionable authenticity.

In conclusion, while "here be dragons" has become a symbol of medieval cartography, it is uncertain whether medieval cartographers regularly wrote this phrase on their maps. Further research and analysis of authentic medieval maps may provide more insight into the use of this phrase during that time period.
